{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 269,
    "dDurationMs": 5081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The many worlds interpretation of quantum mechanics proposes that every time a quantum event gets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5350,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decided, the universe splits so that every\npossible outcome really does occur."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10570,
    "dDurationMs": 5522,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But where exactly are those worlds, and can we ever see them?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20250,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In one branch of the splitting quantum multiverse a radioactive nucleus decay, in another it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25480,
    "dDurationMs": 1059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn’t;"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26539,
    "dDurationMs": 4861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in one “world” a quantum event in your\nbrain produces a neural cascade that leads"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31400,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you to choosing pizza, in another it's salad."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35100,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or at least this is the popular conception\nof Many Worlds: that of a many-branching tree,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40460,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a universe that divides into unthinkably many alternate realities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44450,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked before about this interpretation of quantum mechanics, and whether it’s plausibly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49320,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50640,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "TLDW - it may be but there are problems, and it’s so far untestable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56300,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, I’ll come back to possible ways to test this in an upcoming episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61300,
    "dDurationMs": 5359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today I want to cover something different - If Many Worlds is true - where exactly are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66659,
    "dDurationMs": 1460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those worlds?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68119,
    "dDurationMs": 5331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get to worlds, let’s think about\nponds.C.S Lewis fans will know that ponds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73450,
    "dDurationMs": 4489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can lead to other worlds, but that’s not\nwhat I want to talk about - at least not today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77939,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I want to talk about ripples."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80409,
    "dDurationMs": 5041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you drop a pebble into a pond, a series\nof circular ripples will expand outwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85450,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That pattern will complexify if the ripples\nare, say, broken by a barrier; it’ll be as though"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90430,
    "dDurationMs": 5469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two sources of ripples overlap, leading to\nhigher peaks and lower troughs in the processes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95899,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of constructive and destructive interference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99329,
    "dDurationMs": 2761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Same if the ripples reflect off the edge of\nthe pond."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102090,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or if multiple pebbles are dropped."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104420,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here, we’re seeing the principle of superposition in action."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107810,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In wave mechanics, this principle tells us\nthat when two waves overlap, their amplitude"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113820,
    "dDurationMs": 4359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- in this case the height of the ripple - is\nthe sum of the component waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118179,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the superposition principle also tells\nus something much cooler."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122139,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we want to know how this complex pattern of overlapping ripples will evolve, we only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127189,
    "dDurationMs": 2191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to track the individual ripples."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129380,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each ripple moves as though it’s the only\nwave on the pond."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133050,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can calculate the overlapping pattern\nat any point in time by calculating the motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137310,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of all ripples separately and then adding\nthem together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140740,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the complexity of the sum has no effect on the motion of the individual."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145930,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The weirdness of this is clearer if we watch two waves cross each other in one dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150460,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This complex fluctuation at their collision\nseems to hold no record of the shapes of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156140,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incoming waves, and yet its motion perfectly regenerates those waves, which travel on as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161810,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "though nothing happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163130,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The superposition principle only applies up to a point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166230,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the amplitude of the waves is is too high, the principle can break down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170240,
    "dDurationMs": 4911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for small ripples on a pond, we could\nhave many separate systems of ripples, each"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175151,
    "dDurationMs": 3459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving around as though the pond is otherwise flat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178610,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the risk of getting technical, the superposition principle holds for any linear system - and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183680,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll say more about that another time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186000,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The main point is that this holds approximately for familiar waves, up to some amplitude."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193800,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the superposition principle seems to always hold for the waves that drive quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200460,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the ability for the quantum wavefunction to co-exist and overlap without being affected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206860,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by that overlap is how we’re going to create our many worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210470,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, we are ready to jump through the pond and to explore some worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215620,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by jump through a pond I mean learn some quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219850,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum mechanics is a theory about waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222430,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It tells us that everything in the universe\ncan be described by a wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226590,
    "dDurationMs": 4817,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where a pond ripple is an oscillation in surface height, the wavefunction is an oscillation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231407,
    "dDurationMs": 5603,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in probability, or more accurately probability density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237010,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It represents the shifting distribution of\npossible results you might get if you were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241020,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to try to measure a certain property of a quantum system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244360,
    "dDurationMs": 4461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those measurements appear to be randomly selected based on the current state of the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248821,
    "dDurationMs": 3779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- more likely where the wavefunction is stronger, less where it’s weaker."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252600,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The wavefunction is what underlies our perceived reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256070,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We never see the wavefunction - we only see measurements - we pluck our reality from this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260870,
    "dDurationMs": 2310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fantastically complex structure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263180,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every particle position and momentum and orientation is chosen from a vast array of possibilities,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268870,
    "dDurationMs": 3949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sprinkling of the high points of the cosmic wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272819,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The actual mechanics of quantum mechanics is all about determining the shape and evolution"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277319,
    "dDurationMs": 1511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278830,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To calculate this we use the Schrodinger equation, which tells us how the amplitude of the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283950,
    "dDurationMs": 2730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changes over time and space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286680,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just as with our pond ripples, the wavefunction can overlap and either stack stack up or cancel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291930,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out - constructive or destructive interference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294550,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An example of this behavior is in the double-slit experiment, where the position wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299290,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of an electron passes through two gaps in\na screen and then interferes with itself to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304500,
    "dDurationMs": 2259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produce a complex wavefunction structure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306759,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we try to measure the final location of the electron on a detector screen, we find that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311499,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re more likely to see it where the wavefunction is high - and so electron after electron we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316509,
    "dDurationMs": 2731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trace out these interference bands."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319240,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what decides where the each electron lands?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322340,
    "dDurationMs": 4121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most mainstream interpretation of quantum mechanics - the Copenhagen Interpretation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326461,
    "dDurationMs": 5589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- tells us that the wavefunction “collapses”\n- it instantaneously shrinks from encompassing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332050,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a huge range of possible measurement outcomes to encompassing only the outcome that was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336520,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measured - and that value is selected randomly, but weighted by the pre-measurement wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342110,
    "dDurationMs": 4679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whatever causes this random selection is not the Schrodinger equation - it’s something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346789,
    "dDurationMs": 4171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extra, and Copenhagen doesn’t explain why or when this really happens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350960,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Many Worlds interpretation is rather different."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353240,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It says that the wavefunction never collapses - it evolves forever by the Schrodinger equation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359270,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The wavefunction of the electron joins the\nwavefunction of the detector screen at all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363400,
    "dDurationMs": 1349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "points, rippling onwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364749,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why does an electron end up looking like a single spot on a screen?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369419,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What happens to the rest of its wavefunction?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371809,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand that we have to remember that the electron’s wavefunction is only a tiny"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376889,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sliver of a great cosmic wavefunction that\nincludes every electron and every other particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382349,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383669,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we “see” that the electron hit one\nspot on the screen, what we’re really seeing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387759,
    "dDurationMs": 5571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a cascade of ripples in the cosmic wavefunction, which encompasses the piece-wise wavefunctions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393330,
    "dDurationMs": 4019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of many particles as it makes its way to us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397349,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the wavefunction ripples through the detector, along wires, through computer circuitry, as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403259,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons from the screen, as action potentials down our optical nerves, and finally as signals"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408479,
    "dDurationMs": 1601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our neurons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410080,
    "dDurationMs": 4579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only there does it manifest as a conscious\nawareness of a dot on a screen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414659,
    "dDurationMs": 5473,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Copenhagen interpretation says that at some point in this process, most of the wavefunction vanishes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420132,
    "dDurationMs": 5518,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The paths to the electron's wavefuntion not corresponding to our observation of that spot cease to exist. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425650,
    "dDurationMs": 3212,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many Worlds says the wavefunction persists. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428862,
    "dDurationMs": 3557,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It says that a cascade of ripples for\nevery possible location on the screen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432419,
    "dDurationMs": 5131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "travel down the same wires, through\nthe same circuitry, into the same brain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437550,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They still overlap - stack on top of each\nother - but they no longer interfere, either"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441800,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constructively or destructively."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443380,
    "dDurationMs": 4539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s because the many, many interactions that these wavefunction branches experience on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447919,
    "dDurationMs": 3631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their way to your brain render them forever\ninaccessible to each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451550,
    "dDurationMs": 5119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can see what this means a bit better if\nwe track the experiment backwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456669,
    "dDurationMs": 4541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even prior to hitting the detector screen,\nwe still had many worlds - or at least the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461210,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seeds of them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462210,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, right after passing through the double slit, we had two broad classes of world"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467150,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- one for each slit that the particle may\nhave passed through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471180,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the key is that the wavefunction slice\ncorresponding to those two worlds was still"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475610,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coherent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477250,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means its peaks and troughs could still line up in a systematic way to produce high"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482440,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and low points in the wavefunction - meaningful blips in the probability distribution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488300,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But imagine you placed detector devices in\nfront of those slits to measure which slit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493020,
    "dDurationMs": 1459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electron passed though."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494479,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no way to make a perfectly reliable\nmeasurement without corrupting the phase of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499629,
    "dDurationMs": 5951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction in a way that destroys coherence - destroys the relationship between phases"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505580,
    "dDurationMs": 3149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the wavefunctions emerging from both slits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508729,
    "dDurationMs": 3651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Past the detectors you still have two parts\nof the same electron’s wavefunction, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512380,
    "dDurationMs": 5259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now the phase relationship, the correlation between peaks and troughs, is destroyed "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517639,
    "dDurationMs": 2651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so you don’t see an interference pattern."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520290,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point we may say that the “worlds have split” because there may be no way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525230,
    "dDurationMs": 1470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bring them back into coherence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526700,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked about this decoherence before - and it’s an increasingly favored explanation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531590,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for both the splitting in many worlds or the\napparent collapse in Copenhagen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536420,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once decohered, these “worlds” overlap\nand move through each other, but they can’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542170,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "add together in interesting ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544420,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once there’s no longer a recoverable phase\nrelation between the branches of the wavefunction,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549430,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the worlds have separated forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551500,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the wavefunction of your brain\nalso has branches - different internal states"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556400,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that correspond to each of the branches of\nthe wavefunction from the experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561380,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But those parts of your brain wavefunction\nare out of phase with each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565240,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They match in many ways - including physical location, but there are subtle differences"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569670,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- on one branch a collection of neurons fired to register a spot here, on another branch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a different set of neurons fired leading to\nthe mental experience of a different spot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579520,
    "dDurationMs": 6490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So both mental experiences exist simultaneously, but you only experience one - because you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586010,
    "dDurationMs": 2790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ARE that particular mental experience."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588800,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so you’ve observed the result of the\nexperiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592380,
    "dDurationMs": 2310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Different yous have observed all results."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594690,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now let’s say you record the results of\nthe experiments by tallying spots on two sheets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599580,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of paper - one to your left for electrons\nthat hit the left of the screen, and one to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603390,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your right for right-landing electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605570,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After your visual cortex gets an image of\nthe computer screen, a small slice of your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610390,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brain's wavefunction splits in response to the possible results."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614720,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the split grows, triggering different,\ndecohered responses in your motor cortex."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619930,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately your body’s position wavefunction splits - in one version you move left, in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625470,
    "dDurationMs": 1930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other you move right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627400,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To the left you may bump over your coffee and cleaning it up are late, only just catching"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632460,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your train."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633460,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You don’t find a seat and so end up standing next to where the you of the other branch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637720,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638720,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, I don’t know, maybe next to the love\nof your life, who convinces you to leave research"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643550,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and start a small bed and breakfast in Argentina."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646650,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two lives diverge, and worlds are irreconcilably split."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651650,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But where are they really in relation to each\nother?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656140,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is right-turning you sitting right there on\nthe same train, but out of phase with left-turning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661880,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662880,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, sort of - in the sense that the position\nwavefunctions of the two yous can be mapped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669230,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to these spatial locations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671650,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that sense, we can think of the different\nworlds as separate patterns of ripples on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676660,
    "dDurationMs": 1810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the surface of the pond."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678470,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They overlap in location, but are so hopelessly out of phase that they can never interfere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683520,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with each other - they just pass straight\nthrough."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686320,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our pond analogy, it’s as though the\nripples that make up the other worlds fill"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691050,
    "dDurationMs": 4741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all locations with all possible phases and\nso it's as though those ripples aren’t there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695791,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696791,
    "dDurationMs": 4929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A ripple pattern - a “world” - is only\nthere if you share a phase relation with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701720,
    "dDurationMs": 4521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You become entangled with the final state of the electron - correlated with it - one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706241,
    "dDurationMs": 4499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "version of you maps only to the right-landing electron, one only to the left."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710740,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now I’m not saying Many Worlds is right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713960,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Certainly some ways in which its represented are definitely wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717830,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, worlds don’t split constantly\nand irreversibly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721330,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On a quantum scale, worlds - or wavefunction components - recombine all the time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727020,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Splitting happens when phase relations are\nscrambled due to interactions - and that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730400,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- the entire universe doesn’t\nsplit with every atomic wiggle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734340,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many Worlds should also not be taken on faith - we should keep trying to come up with tests."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739670,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And one idea does exist - which may allow us to actually send messages between worlds - assuming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745830,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746830,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s for another time - and I’ll\ndo my best to bring it to every future branch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751100,
    "dDurationMs": 3199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our splitting quantum space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
